WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.191 --> 00:00:08.141
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:10.061
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.061 --> 00:00:12.921
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.931 --> 00:00:14.571
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.571 --> 00:00:17.181
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.191 --> 00:00:19.131
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.131 --> 00:00:20.841
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.251 --> 00:00:23.521
I'm Alastair Budge, and today

00:00:23.691 --> 00:00:26.041
we are going to talk about James

00:00:26.051 --> 00:00:28.381
Boswell and Samuel Johnson.

00:00:28.941 --> 00:00:31.201
It's a story about a strange

00:00:31.211 --> 00:00:33.721
friendship between two very different

00:00:33.731 --> 00:00:37.351
men, and about how one insecure

00:00:37.371 --> 00:00:40.321
young Scotsman changed the way we

00:00:40.321 --> 00:00:42.261
write about other people’s lives.

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:44.725
So, let's not waste a minute

00:00:45.001 --> 00:00:46.941
and get right into it.

00:00:48.461 --> 00:00:51.275
It is November 1762.

00:00:51.811 --> 00:00:54.981
James Boswell is twenty-two years old,

00:00:55.231 --> 00:00:57.921
and he has just arrived in London.

00:00:59.241 --> 00:01:01.551
He is not yet famous for anything.

00:01:02.091 --> 00:01:05.201
He is the son of a Scottish lord, which

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:07.931
gives him a name and a certain kind of

00:01:07.941 --> 00:01:11.571
introduction in polite company, but not

00:01:11.581 --> 00:01:14.851
much money and no particular prospects.

00:01:15.371 --> 00:01:17.451
He is charming, good-looking,

00:01:17.781 --> 00:01:20.331
and consumed by an ambition

00:01:20.521 --> 00:01:22.931
of a vague and restless kind.

00:01:24.391 --> 00:01:26.341
From the moment he arrives,

00:01:26.521 --> 00:01:28.571
he writes everything down.

00:01:29.271 --> 00:01:31.391
It's not a boring list of what he

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:33.241
had for lunch or who he saw at the

00:01:33.241 --> 00:01:35.341
theatre, although he does go into

00:01:35.341 --> 00:01:37.151
plenty of those sorts of details.

00:01:37.911 --> 00:01:41.491
It is a frank and often funny account

00:01:41.771 --> 00:01:44.251
of what it felt like to be a young man

00:01:44.531 --> 00:01:47.141
trying to make his mark on a city he

00:01:47.141 --> 00:01:49.431
has dreamt about since he was a boy.

00:01:50.951 --> 00:01:52.751
He writes about parties,

00:01:52.761 --> 00:01:54.951
theatres, dinners, and the

00:01:54.951 --> 00:01:56.581
people he wants to impress.

00:01:57.211 --> 00:02:00.481
But he also writes about the things most

00:02:00.481 --> 00:02:02.941
men of his class would have carefully

00:02:02.951 --> 00:02:05.831
hidden: the intimate details of his

00:02:05.831 --> 00:02:08.711
encounters with prostitutes on Westminster

00:02:08.711 --> 00:02:11.721
Bridge, a conversation where he accuses

00:02:11.741 --> 00:02:14.411
a lover of having given him gonorrhoea.

00:02:15.321 --> 00:02:17.921
He writes about falling in love with

00:02:17.921 --> 00:02:20.491
women who do not love him back, about

00:02:20.521 --> 00:02:22.941
his recurrent bouts of low spirits,

00:02:23.231 --> 00:02:25.781
his deep sense of guilt about not

00:02:25.801 --> 00:02:28.021
being able to restrain his libido,

00:02:28.431 --> 00:02:31.171
and about his fear that he will never

00:02:31.211 --> 00:02:33.471
become the person he wants to be.

00:02:34.431 --> 00:02:36.981
His words were never intended for

00:02:36.991 --> 00:02:39.731
an external audience, and indeed

00:02:39.731 --> 00:02:41.751
his journals weren't discovered

00:02:41.921 --> 00:02:45.011
until the 1920s, 150 years later.

00:02:45.741 --> 00:02:49.961
But in those pages you can already see the

00:02:49.961 --> 00:02:53.471
gift that would later make him famous: the

00:02:53.471 --> 00:02:57.291
ability to watch life closely, remember

00:02:57.291 --> 00:03:01.811
speech, and turn personality into prose.

00:03:02.991 --> 00:03:06.261
Now, before we get more into Boswell

00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:08.911
the writer, we need to understand

00:03:08.931 --> 00:03:11.131
the world he has walked into.

00:03:12.331 --> 00:03:15.701
In 1762, we are bang in the

00:03:15.711 --> 00:03:17.691
middle of the Enlightenment.

00:03:18.421 --> 00:03:21.221
London is a city of roughly three

00:03:21.221 --> 00:03:22.751
quarters of a million people.

00:03:23.401 --> 00:03:25.481
It is the largest city in Europe,

00:03:25.771 --> 00:03:28.521
and it is loud, overcrowded,

00:03:28.771 --> 00:03:31.091
and seething with ideas.

00:03:32.021 --> 00:03:33.711
The coffee house is the

00:03:33.761 --> 00:03:35.941
engine of intellectual life.

00:03:36.471 --> 00:03:38.921
Merchants gossip about trade.

00:03:39.111 --> 00:03:41.181
Politicians argue about

00:03:41.191 --> 00:03:42.591
the American colonies.

00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:44.681
Writers read each other's

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:47.311
work aloud and tear it apart.

00:03:48.021 --> 00:03:49.341
There are also clubs.

00:03:49.681 --> 00:03:52.671
Literary clubs, dining clubs, debating

00:03:52.671 --> 00:03:54.921
societies where men argue about

00:03:54.921 --> 00:03:57.231
philosophy for hours and then walk

00:03:57.241 --> 00:04:00.231
home through streets ankle-deep in mud.

00:04:01.211 --> 00:04:02.501
The professional writer

00:04:02.991 --> 00:04:04.451
is a new kind of creature.

00:04:05.121 --> 00:04:06.991
Before this, writers

00:04:07.081 --> 00:04:08.911
depended on wealthy patrons.

00:04:09.451 --> 00:04:12.331
Now, increasingly, they write for the

00:04:12.331 --> 00:04:15.941
public, which means they live and die by

00:04:15.941 --> 00:04:18.681
whether the public buys what they produce.

00:04:19.621 --> 00:04:21.481
Into this world, young

00:04:21.521 --> 00:04:24.201
Boswell walks, diary in hand.

00:04:25.901 --> 00:04:28.271
The man he most wants to meet

00:04:28.431 --> 00:04:30.381
is called Samuel Johnson.

00:04:31.791 --> 00:04:34.821
If this is ringing some bells and you

00:04:34.821 --> 00:04:37.001
are a particularly dedicated listener

00:04:37.001 --> 00:04:40.281
to this show, you may remember that back

00:04:40.291 --> 00:04:44.211
in March 2020 we made an episode about

00:04:44.291 --> 00:04:47.121
Johnson’s dictionary, the most famous

00:04:47.241 --> 00:04:49.301
early dictionary of the English language.

00:04:49.621 --> 00:04:52.011
It's episode number 39, if you'd like

00:04:52.011 --> 00:04:53.621
to listen to that one afterwards.

00:04:54.121 --> 00:04:57.381
But if you haven't, or as a reminder,

00:04:57.871 --> 00:05:00.071
here's the background on Johnson.

00:05:01.025 --> 00:05:04.755
He was born in 1709 in the English

00:05:04.755 --> 00:05:07.385
Midlands, so he is thirty-one

00:05:07.395 --> 00:05:09.005
years older than Boswell.

00:05:09.895 --> 00:05:12.595
He was an incredibly bright young boy,

00:05:12.995 --> 00:05:16.505
and won a place at Oxford, only to have

00:05:16.505 --> 00:05:19.105
to drop out after his first year because

00:05:19.105 --> 00:05:21.525
his parents could not afford the fees.

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:24.335
He moved to London in his late

00:05:24.335 --> 00:05:27.055
twenties with almost nothing, and

00:05:27.255 --> 00:05:29.815
spent the next two decades writing

00:05:29.825 --> 00:05:32.105
for newspapers and magazines, but

00:05:32.605 --> 00:05:34.595
essentially living in poverty.

00:05:35.875 --> 00:05:39.855
Then, in 1755, he got his big

00:05:39.875 --> 00:05:42.245
break, with the publication of his

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:44.375
Dictionary of the English Language.

00:05:45.235 --> 00:05:47.835
He had worked on it for nine years.

00:05:48.275 --> 00:05:50.305
It was not the first English dictionary,

00:05:50.655 --> 00:05:54.285
but it was far and away the most thorough,

00:05:54.615 --> 00:05:57.785
the most witty, and the most alive.

00:05:59.185 --> 00:06:02.015
And almost overnight, it made him

00:06:02.025 --> 00:06:05.295
famous, but not immediately rich.

00:06:05.745 --> 00:06:08.415
That came afterwards, it was only

00:06:08.415 --> 00:06:12.265
in 1762, seven years later, that he

00:06:12.265 --> 00:06:15.055
received a pension from the Crown, and

00:06:15.345 --> 00:06:18.145
for the first time in his adult life

00:06:18.585 --> 00:06:20.545
he did not have to worry about money.

00:06:21.905 --> 00:06:23.535
He is free to think,

00:06:23.665 --> 00:06:26.025
to talk, to be himself.

00:06:26.935 --> 00:06:30.335
And who is he, who is Samuel Johnson?

00:06:31.095 --> 00:06:33.345
Well, he is quite the character.

00:06:34.175 --> 00:06:37.265
Physically, he is immediately striking.

00:06:37.925 --> 00:06:40.585
He is large, tall and overweight,

00:06:40.875 --> 00:06:42.775
and his face is covered in

00:06:42.785 --> 00:06:45.405
scars from a childhood disease.

00:06:45.945 --> 00:06:48.895
He is partly blind and partly deaf.

00:06:49.485 --> 00:06:51.835
He rocks backwards and forwards when

00:06:51.835 --> 00:06:54.595
he thinks, makes odd noises under

00:06:54.595 --> 00:06:57.853
his breath, and has this superstition

00:06:58.155 --> 00:07:00.285
that makes him touch every wooden

00:07:00.285 --> 00:07:02.195
post he passes in the street.

00:07:03.565 --> 00:07:06.095
He is deeply melancholic, and

00:07:06.455 --> 00:07:08.975
were he around today, he might

00:07:08.975 --> 00:07:11.255
well be diagnosed with clinical

00:07:11.255 --> 00:07:13.585
depression or bipolar disorder.

00:07:14.675 --> 00:07:16.445
He got married at the age of

00:07:16.445 --> 00:07:19.835
twenty-five, to a widow of forty-six.

00:07:20.805 --> 00:07:23.865
It is an unusual match, but he remains

00:07:23.875 --> 00:07:26.745
dedicated to her, and even after her

00:07:26.755 --> 00:07:29.815
death, when he is only forty-two, he

00:07:29.815 --> 00:07:32.815
refuses to remarry and continues to

00:07:32.815 --> 00:07:35.105
mourn her for the rest of his life.

00:07:36.315 --> 00:07:38.945
He is, in other words, a man

00:07:39.175 --> 00:07:41.545
of extraordinary peculiarities.

00:07:42.525 --> 00:07:45.095
But he is incredibly intelligent,

00:07:45.395 --> 00:07:48.205
great company, and known as the

00:07:48.215 --> 00:07:50.715
finest conversationalist in England.

00:07:51.895 --> 00:07:54.405
In practice, this means he likes

00:07:54.405 --> 00:07:57.075
to debate, to argue a point.

00:07:57.975 --> 00:08:02.005
And when Johnson argues, he argues to win.

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:04.715
He does not aim for nuance

00:08:04.735 --> 00:08:06.655
in conversation; he aims

00:08:06.805 --> 00:08:08.395
for total demolition.

00:08:09.055 --> 00:08:11.385
He will defend a position he does not

00:08:11.395 --> 00:08:13.985
privately hold simply because he finds

00:08:13.985 --> 00:08:15.875
it more interesting to argue that way.

00:08:16.885 --> 00:08:20.535
Now, this doesn't make him mean or nasty,

00:08:20.855 --> 00:08:22.855
unkind to those less fortunate than him.

00:08:23.365 --> 00:08:26.305
He is famously charitable, and there

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:28.185
is one account of him giving his

00:08:28.185 --> 00:08:30.755
last coins to a beggar asleep in the

00:08:30.755 --> 00:08:32.215
street in the middle of the night,

00:08:32.635 --> 00:08:34.475
sitting beside the man until he

00:08:34.475 --> 00:08:36.915
woke up, and then giving him more.

00:08:37.705 --> 00:08:40.685
He is a walking bundle of contradictions.

00:08:41.375 --> 00:08:44.015
Which, as it turns out, makes him

00:08:44.015 --> 00:08:46.785
the ideal subject for a biography.

00:08:47.945 --> 00:08:50.005
Now, back to Boswell.

00:08:50.865 --> 00:08:53.275
James Boswell is the eldest son

00:08:53.485 --> 00:08:55.455
of a Scottish judge with high

00:08:55.455 --> 00:08:57.605
expectations and very little

00:08:57.655 --> 00:09:00.603
patience for his son's enthusiasms.

00:09:01.055 --> 00:09:03.035
Their relationship is one of the

00:09:03.035 --> 00:09:06.365
great tensions of Boswell's life: his

00:09:06.365 --> 00:09:09.085
father wants him to be a respectable

00:09:09.095 --> 00:09:12.145
Scottish lawyer; Boswell wants to be

00:09:12.145 --> 00:09:15.365
a writer, a wit, a man about town.

00:09:16.275 --> 00:09:18.675
He wants to be taken seriously in London.

00:09:19.205 --> 00:09:22.485
He wants, if he is honest, to be famous.

00:09:23.715 --> 00:09:26.375
But he is deeply flawed, as he

00:09:26.375 --> 00:09:27.975
admits to himself in his diary.

00:09:28.635 --> 00:09:29.485
He is vain.

00:09:29.495 --> 00:09:31.095
He drinks too much.

00:09:31.245 --> 00:09:33.555
He is always visiting prostitutes,

00:09:33.715 --> 00:09:35.585
feeling terrible about what he has done

00:09:35.875 --> 00:09:38.385
and praying for forgiveness, and then

00:09:38.625 --> 00:09:40.425
doing the same thing all over again.

00:09:41.145 --> 00:09:44.665
He falls in love impulsively and unwisely.

00:09:45.435 --> 00:09:48.485
He is, by many measures, a mess.

00:09:49.715 --> 00:09:52.325
The extraordinary thing is that Boswell

00:09:52.585 --> 00:09:54.405
does not hide this from himself.

00:09:54.895 --> 00:09:57.015
He records his vanity and weakness

00:09:57.355 --> 00:09:59.735
almost as carefully as he records

00:09:59.805 --> 00:10:01.135
other people’s brilliance.

00:10:02.065 --> 00:10:05.705
But he has a unique talent, though at

00:10:05.705 --> 00:10:08.496
first he does not seem fully aware of it.

00:10:09.605 --> 00:10:13.065
He has an incredible ability to observe

00:10:13.095 --> 00:10:15.835
a situation, notice the smallest

00:10:15.835 --> 00:10:17.585
details that other people might

00:10:17.585 --> 00:10:21.115
miss, and then go home and write the

00:10:21.115 --> 00:10:23.815
entire thing down, reconstructing the

00:10:23.815 --> 00:10:26.445
conversation from memory, sometimes

00:10:26.755 --> 00:10:29.985
hours of talk, in extraordinary detail.

00:10:31.205 --> 00:10:33.725
And he is not sitting silently

00:10:33.735 --> 00:10:35.765
in the corner like a note-taker.

00:10:36.105 --> 00:10:39.235
He is in the conversation, laughing,

00:10:39.295 --> 00:10:42.045
flattering, interrupting, being insulted,

00:10:42.485 --> 00:10:44.627
and then going home to reconstruct it.

00:10:45.865 --> 00:10:47.875
This is the talent that will

00:10:47.895 --> 00:10:50.475
produce, eventually, the greatest

00:10:50.475 --> 00:10:52.705
biography in the English language.

00:10:53.175 --> 00:10:55.805
But first, he needs his subject.

00:10:56.575 --> 00:10:59.245
And you will win no prizes for

00:10:59.245 --> 00:11:00.655
guessing who it's going to be.

00:11:01.811 --> 00:11:04.431
The first meeting between Boswell and

00:11:04.431 --> 00:11:09.490
Johnson happens on the 16th of May, 1763.

00:11:10.031 --> 00:11:13.231
Boswell is in the back parlour of a

00:11:13.241 --> 00:11:15.613
bookshop in Covent Garden when Samuel

00:11:15.613 --> 00:11:17.243
Johnson walks through the door.

00:11:18.212 --> 00:11:19.923
Boswell has been trying to

00:11:19.973 --> 00:11:22.163
engineer this meeting for weeks.

00:11:22.543 --> 00:11:24.323
It's now eight years since the

00:11:24.343 --> 00:11:26.353
publication of the famous dictionary,

00:11:26.693 --> 00:11:29.413
and Johnson is the most celebrated

00:11:29.423 --> 00:11:31.113
literary figure in London.

00:11:32.063 --> 00:11:34.313
Boswell is a young Scotsman

00:11:34.613 --> 00:11:35.783
who has published nothing.

00:11:36.443 --> 00:11:38.443
He is nervous, not least

00:11:38.613 --> 00:11:40.053
because he knows that Johnson is

00:11:40.053 --> 00:11:42.463
famously rude about the Scots.

00:11:43.933 --> 00:11:45.993
The owner of the bookshop, Thomas

00:11:45.993 --> 00:11:48.003
Davies, makes the introduction.

00:11:49.453 --> 00:11:52.293
Boswell tries to be funny, confronting

00:11:52.293 --> 00:11:54.703
the Scottish elephant in the room head-on,

00:11:55.063 --> 00:11:58.153
saying "Mr Johnson, I do indeed come

00:11:58.153 --> 00:12:00.383
from Scotland, but I cannot help it."

00:12:01.695 --> 00:12:05.243
Johnson looks at him, and says, deadpan:

00:12:06.103 --> 00:12:08.513
"That, Sir, is what a very great

00:12:08.513 --> 00:12:10.473
many of your countrymen cannot help."

00:12:11.808 --> 00:12:14.718
It is not an auspicious start, and Boswell

00:12:14.718 --> 00:12:18.118
is, in his own words, "much disconcerted."

00:12:19.438 --> 00:12:22.178
But this will be the start

00:12:22.178 --> 00:12:24.458
of one of the most improbable

00:12:24.458 --> 00:12:26.468
friendships in literary history.

00:12:27.208 --> 00:12:28.528
Johnson is fifty-three,

00:12:28.918 --> 00:12:30.408
Boswell is twenty-two.

00:12:30.908 --> 00:12:34.348
Johnson is English; Boswell is Scottish,

00:12:34.548 --> 00:12:36.368
which is a significant handicap

00:12:36.618 --> 00:12:38.228
in this particular relationship.

00:12:38.968 --> 00:12:41.648
Johnson is a man of deep religious

00:12:41.678 --> 00:12:45.338
conviction and apparent sexual abstinence

00:12:45.438 --> 00:12:48.408
after the death of his wife; Boswell

00:12:48.628 --> 00:12:50.938
is a regular among the prostitutes

00:12:50.958 --> 00:12:53.788
of Westminster Bridge, and seemingly

00:12:53.878 --> 00:12:55.948
incapable of changing his behaviour.

00:12:56.688 --> 00:12:58.968
Johnson is the city's most famous

00:12:58.968 --> 00:13:01.858
writer; Boswell is essentially

00:13:01.998 --> 00:13:03.858
a tourist and a nobody.

00:13:04.738 --> 00:13:07.168
And yet they form one of the most

00:13:07.198 --> 00:13:09.708
unlikely friendships in literary history.

00:13:10.878 --> 00:13:13.978
Much has been written as to why, and

00:13:14.218 --> 00:13:15.598
you can of course come to your own

00:13:15.598 --> 00:13:17.778
conclusion by reading the incredibly

00:13:17.778 --> 00:13:19.768
detailed accounts Boswell kept of

00:13:19.768 --> 00:13:21.458
their two-decade-long friendship.

00:13:22.718 --> 00:13:24.918
There is surely an element of

00:13:25.108 --> 00:13:27.271
father-son relationship to it.

00:13:28.038 --> 00:13:30.288
Johnson is thirty-one years older,

00:13:30.528 --> 00:13:33.018
with no family or children of his own.

00:13:33.738 --> 00:13:35.778
Boswell has a very difficult

00:13:35.798 --> 00:13:37.698
relationship with his own father,

00:13:38.048 --> 00:13:40.538
and is far happier in Johnson's

00:13:40.538 --> 00:13:42.758
company than he is in his father's.

00:13:43.808 --> 00:13:46.708
For Boswell, being around the incredibly

00:13:46.708 --> 00:13:49.968
witty Johnson is clearly very enjoyable.

00:13:50.418 --> 00:13:52.378
He takes great pride in recording

00:13:52.378 --> 00:13:54.468
how Johnson had ridiculed someone

00:13:54.468 --> 00:13:57.538
over dinner, and is even happy to

00:13:57.538 --> 00:14:00.028
record how Johnson insults him,

00:14:00.448 --> 00:14:01.968
as long as it is witty enough.

00:14:03.458 --> 00:14:06.528
And for Johnson, well, he can

00:14:06.528 --> 00:14:09.128
see that Boswell adores him, and

00:14:09.418 --> 00:14:11.008
who doesn't like to feel wanted?

00:14:12.428 --> 00:14:14.067
Now, the friendship was

00:14:14.087 --> 00:14:15.468
not always continuous.

00:14:15.988 --> 00:14:17.768
After their first meetings in London,

00:14:18.168 --> 00:14:20.488
Boswell spent long periods away:

00:14:20.768 --> 00:14:22.968
back in Scotland, completing the

00:14:22.978 --> 00:14:25.158
legal training his father demanded,

00:14:25.338 --> 00:14:26.988
as well as travelling in Europe.

00:14:27.898 --> 00:14:30.918
In Europe he pursued famous men with

00:14:30.918 --> 00:14:33.188
the same intensity with which he pursued

00:14:33.218 --> 00:14:35.898
Johnson, meeting Voltaire and Jean-Jacques

00:14:35.898 --> 00:14:39.228
Rousseau, and he even found time for

00:14:39.228 --> 00:14:41.708
a brief affair with Rousseau’s lover.

00:14:42.698 --> 00:14:45.528
But whenever he could, Boswell would

00:14:45.528 --> 00:14:48.458
come to London, and there the primary

00:14:48.468 --> 00:14:50.678
attraction was Samuel Johnson.

00:14:51.498 --> 00:14:52.758
He takes notes.

00:14:52.838 --> 00:14:54.728
He records conversations.

00:14:55.048 --> 00:14:56.958
He writes down Johnson's views

00:14:57.098 --> 00:14:59.088
on everything from the American

00:14:59.088 --> 00:15:01.288
Revolutionary War to the existence of

00:15:01.318 --> 00:15:04.518
ghosts to the proper way to eat a peach.

00:15:06.058 --> 00:15:08.078
Johnson's feelings about Scotland

00:15:08.278 --> 00:15:10.268
and the Scots are a constant

00:15:10.368 --> 00:15:12.178
undercurrent in their friendship.

00:15:13.468 --> 00:15:16.308
To give you a few examples: "The noblest

00:15:16.378 --> 00:15:19.058
prospect which a Scotchman ever sees,"

00:15:19.288 --> 00:15:21.988
Johnson says, "is the high road that leads

00:15:22.007 --> 00:15:26.547
him to England." Or "Much may be made

00:15:26.567 --> 00:15:28.958
of a Scotchman, if he be caught young."

00:15:30.868 --> 00:15:33.508
Johnson is perennially rude about

00:15:33.508 --> 00:15:36.408
Scotland and the Scots, and it seems

00:15:36.418 --> 00:15:38.878
Boswell takes this as a challenge

00:15:39.088 --> 00:15:41.228
to bring Johnson to his home country

00:15:41.538 --> 00:15:43.428
and get him to see for himself.

00:15:44.848 --> 00:15:48.118
In the summer of 1773, he manages,

00:15:48.408 --> 00:15:49.888
and Johnson agrees to go on

00:15:49.888 --> 00:15:51.858
a tour through Scotland, with

00:15:51.868 --> 00:15:53.998
Boswell as his travelling partner.

00:15:55.218 --> 00:15:57.048
It is one of the more unlikely

00:15:57.058 --> 00:15:59.168
journeys in British literary history.

00:15:59.688 --> 00:16:02.538
Samuel Johnson is sixty-three, has

00:16:02.538 --> 00:16:04.688
spent most of his adult life in London

00:16:04.948 --> 00:16:07.018
and its immediate surroundings, and

00:16:07.288 --> 00:16:09.448
appears to have little interest in

00:16:09.478 --> 00:16:11.268
anything beyond the English border.

00:16:12.208 --> 00:16:14.098
He agrees to travel with Boswell

00:16:14.378 --> 00:16:15.868
through the Scottish Highlands

00:16:16.108 --> 00:16:18.128
and out to the Western Isles.

00:16:19.058 --> 00:16:20.448
They set off in August.

00:16:20.798 --> 00:16:22.668
They travel through Edinburgh, where

00:16:22.668 --> 00:16:24.858
Johnson is, to his slight surprise,

00:16:25.198 --> 00:16:27.198
received with enormous respect.

00:16:27.738 --> 00:16:30.218
They visit St Andrews and Aberdeen.

00:16:30.468 --> 00:16:31.898
They push north into the

00:16:31.908 --> 00:16:34.218
Highlands and then west, taking

00:16:34.218 --> 00:16:36.228
a boat to the Isle of Skye.

00:16:37.238 --> 00:16:39.798
The landscape does something to Johnson.

00:16:40.578 --> 00:16:41.548
He has never seen

00:16:41.578 --> 00:16:43.507
anything remotely like it.

00:16:43.888 --> 00:16:46.668
The vast, treeless mountains,

00:16:46.918 --> 00:16:49.288
the lochs, the sheer emptiness.

00:16:50.928 --> 00:16:53.898
He is also deeply moved by the poverty

00:16:53.898 --> 00:16:56.608
he sees, and the ruins of the old

00:16:56.658 --> 00:16:59.358
clan system, which is already being

00:16:59.358 --> 00:17:02.008
dismantled after the Jacobite rebellions.

00:17:03.378 --> 00:17:04.868
They make it out to the island

00:17:04.868 --> 00:17:06.958
of Iona, which is one of the most

00:17:06.998 --> 00:17:08.798
ancient Christian sites in Britain,

00:17:09.198 --> 00:17:11.648
where the abbey stands half-ruined

00:17:11.888 --> 00:17:14.198
among the graves of Scottish kings.

00:17:15.418 --> 00:17:17.248
Johnson, standing there,

00:17:17.748 --> 00:17:19.458
is quiet for a long time.

00:17:21.108 --> 00:17:23.648
"That man is little to be envied,"

00:17:23.998 --> 00:17:26.568
he says eventually, "whose patriotism

00:17:26.848 --> 00:17:29.508
would not gain force upon the plain

00:17:29.518 --> 00:17:32.338
of Marathon, or whose piety would not

00:17:32.348 --> 00:17:34.878
grow warmer among the ruins of Iona."

00:17:36.348 --> 00:17:38.768
In other words, being physically

00:17:38.778 --> 00:17:41.458
in a place changes you, makes you

00:17:41.708 --> 00:17:44.278
see and feel things differently.

00:17:45.328 --> 00:17:47.518
And this is quite some admission

00:17:47.828 --> 00:17:49.708
from a man who had previously

00:17:49.708 --> 00:17:51.218
said the best thing a Scotsman

00:17:51.218 --> 00:17:53.658
could see was the road to England.

00:17:55.168 --> 00:17:58.206
Now, this was in late 1773.

00:17:58.638 --> 00:18:01.578
Johnson would live for another 11 years,

00:18:01.918 --> 00:18:04.608
but the end of his life was characterised

00:18:04.628 --> 00:18:06.678
by ill health and depression.

00:18:08.198 --> 00:18:10.678
Johnson knew that his young friend had

00:18:10.678 --> 00:18:12.978
been keeping notes, and was planning

00:18:12.978 --> 00:18:15.078
to publish an account of his life,

00:18:15.478 --> 00:18:18.408
but he would have had no idea on quite

00:18:18.468 --> 00:18:20.918
how important it would be, and that

00:18:20.918 --> 00:18:23.218
it would come to be regarded as one of

00:18:23.218 --> 00:18:25.808
the greatest biographies ever written.

00:18:27.268 --> 00:18:31.808
Johnson died in 1784, and it wasn't

00:18:31.838 --> 00:18:35.198
until 1791, seven years after

00:18:35.198 --> 00:18:37.748
his death, that Boswell published

00:18:37.778 --> 00:18:39.748
his account of his friend's life.

00:18:40.708 --> 00:18:43.238
It wasn't the first biography of Samuel

00:18:43.238 --> 00:18:46.108
Johnson — several others have been rushed

00:18:46.108 --> 00:18:48.438
out to capitalise on the public interest

00:18:48.718 --> 00:18:50.718
in the immediate aftermath of his death.

00:18:51.608 --> 00:18:54.298
But it is certainly the best,

00:18:54.618 --> 00:18:56.788
and it became the blueprint

00:18:57.018 --> 00:18:58.568
for the modern biography.

00:19:00.098 --> 00:19:02.858
Before Boswell, and before The

00:19:02.858 --> 00:19:05.608
Life of Samuel Johnson, biography

00:19:05.988 --> 00:19:08.418
often had a fairly clear purpose.

00:19:09.118 --> 00:19:11.328
You wrote about what a great person

00:19:11.338 --> 00:19:14.268
did: their battles, their political

00:19:14.268 --> 00:19:16.838
achievements, their public acts.

00:19:17.298 --> 00:19:19.678
You presented them as an example

00:19:19.678 --> 00:19:22.338
to admire or a warning to others.

00:19:23.338 --> 00:19:24.928
You did not typically record the

00:19:24.928 --> 00:19:26.828
moment they were rude to someone

00:19:26.828 --> 00:19:29.108
at dinner, or the odd things they

00:19:29.118 --> 00:19:31.288
said when they were only half-paying

00:19:31.288 --> 00:19:33.398
attention, or the way their voice

00:19:33.398 --> 00:19:35.158
changed when they were defending

00:19:35.158 --> 00:19:37.098
something they privately doubted.

00:19:38.348 --> 00:19:41.298
Boswell records all of this.

00:19:42.358 --> 00:19:44.078
His account runs to more

00:19:44.078 --> 00:19:45.698
than twelve hundred pages.

00:19:46.057 --> 00:19:48.738
It's built on twenty years of notes,

00:19:49.138 --> 00:19:52.088
letters, and reconstructed conversations.

00:19:52.578 --> 00:19:54.948
You can hear the words coming off

00:19:54.948 --> 00:19:57.558
the page, the very words Samuel

00:19:57.558 --> 00:20:00.348
Johnson spoke, not summarised, not

00:20:00.348 --> 00:20:03.278
paraphrased, but actually spoken.

00:20:04.398 --> 00:20:06.448
You learn that Johnson believed

00:20:06.538 --> 00:20:08.308
there was no point eating boiled

00:20:08.318 --> 00:20:10.708
mutton unless it fell from the bone.

00:20:11.518 --> 00:20:13.888
You learn that he was terrified of

00:20:13.888 --> 00:20:16.638
death, that he prayed in private

00:20:16.948 --> 00:20:19.678
with intense fervour, that he was

00:20:19.678 --> 00:20:21.868
capable of cruelty towards people

00:20:21.868 --> 00:20:24.598
he found dull and extraordinary

00:20:24.598 --> 00:20:26.708
generosity towards strangers.

00:20:28.268 --> 00:20:30.828
You learn that his opinions were

00:20:30.828 --> 00:20:33.088
delivered with complete confidence

00:20:33.348 --> 00:20:35.848
and often deliberate provocation.

00:20:36.698 --> 00:20:38.528
"Patriotism is the last

00:20:38.528 --> 00:20:39.978
refuge of a scoundrel."

00:20:40.888 --> 00:20:42.378
"When a man is tired of

00:20:42.378 --> 00:20:44.358
London, he is tired of life."

00:20:45.498 --> 00:20:47.128
Judged by modern standards,

00:20:47.488 --> 00:20:50.148
Johnson can seem both shockingly

00:20:50.228 --> 00:20:53.058
bigoted and strikingly enlightened.

00:20:54.048 --> 00:20:56.208
On the Americans seeking independence,

00:20:56.478 --> 00:20:58.198
he was firmly against it.

00:20:58.748 --> 00:21:01.798
On slavery, he was fiercely opposed.

00:21:02.598 --> 00:21:05.168
On the possibility of a woman preaching as

00:21:05.168 --> 00:21:08.458
well as a man: "Sir, a woman's preaching

00:21:08.658 --> 00:21:10.708
is like a dog's walking on his hinder

00:21:10.708 --> 00:21:13.558
legs. It is not done well; but you

00:21:13.558 --> 00:21:15.573
are surprised to find it done at all."

00:21:16.738 --> 00:21:19.160
Hinder legs, by the way, is an old way

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:22.330
of saying "hind" legs, or back legs.

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:25.660
In other words, Johnson is saying that a

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:28.560
woman preacher is like a dog walking on

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:32.030
its back legs: not good, not natural, but

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:34.550
surprising enough to make people look.

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:37.110
It is cruel, sexist and

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:38.470
offensive, certainly.

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:42.280
But it is also pure Johnson: compressed,

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:46.170
comic, brutal, and impossible to forget.

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:50.880
And this is what Boswell understood: A

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:54.100
biography should not simply record events.

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:56.350
It should reveal character.

00:21:56.890 --> 00:21:59.030
You should come away feeling not

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:01.330
only that you know what a person

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:03.940
did, but that you know what it was

00:22:03.950 --> 00:22:05.930
like to sit in a room with them.

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:08.450
And in Johnson's case, that you had

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:11.670
been insulted by him, been irritated

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:14.580
by him, laughed at his jokes, watched

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:16.810
him defend something he was privately

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:19.200
not sure about simply because no one in

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:21.320
the room had yet managed to defeat him.

00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:24.940
Johnson himself was, in theory,

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:27.460
opposed to this kind of biography.

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:29.790
He thought private life

00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:31.080
should remain private.

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:34.580
He thought biographers intruded too far.

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:37.870
He did not, perhaps, fully understand

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:41.060
what Boswell was doing, even as Boswell

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:43.460
was doing it right in front of him.

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:47.990
So, to wrap up, Johnson lives

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:50.330
today, more vividly than almost

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:52.230
any other figure of his century,

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:55.200
because of exactly that intrusion.

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:59.030
Because Boswell refused to treat greatness

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:01.520
as something smooth and respectable.

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:03.840
He showed the contradictions.

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:06.820
He showed the vanity, the fear, the

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:09.970
tenderness, the cruelty, the jokes, the

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:12.870
odd habits, and the flashes of genius.

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:16.450
And we know all of this because,

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:20.900
in November 1762, a young, anxious,

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:22.810
ambitious, somewhat dissolute

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:25.980
Scotsman arrived in London with a

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:28.880
notebook, a hunger for fame, and

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:31.290
a rare gift for paying attention.

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:35.440
OK then, that is it for today's episode

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:38.320
on James Boswell, Samuel Johnson, and

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:40.350
the strange friendship behind one of

00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:42.150
the greatest biographies ever written.

00:23:42.650 --> 00:23:43.980
Whether or not you’ve listened to that

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:46.910
old episode from 2020, I hope this gave

00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:49.220
you a new way to think about Johnson,

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:51.263
Boswell, and the strange friendship

00:23:51.473 --> 00:23:53.313
between these two peculiar men.

00:23:54.003 --> 00:23:56.063
You've been listening to English Learning

00:23:56.063 --> 00:23:58.723
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:23:59.103 --> 00:24:02.143
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:02.153 --> 00:24:04.583
I'll catch you in the next episode.

